\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Layer selection as researcher degree of freedom.}
Layer selection is an underexamined methodological choice in the probing literature that can silently determine a study's conclusions. Our five-metric ablation (\cref{sec:layer-results}) demonstrates that a single metric component, the crossing rate, reverses the central finding: at biased layers, the HS advantage appears scale-dependent; at corrected layers, all four models are significant (\cref{sec:evaluation}). The mechanism is representation compression at shallow layers (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), which pushes a linear probe to the noise floor and inflates crossing rate independently of discriminative power. Any composite metric that incorporates crossing rate is susceptible to this bias, and we hypothesize the effect generalizes beyond safety probing to any threshold-based detection study. We recommend that probing studies report results under at least two complementary layer selection criteria and that multi-metric ablations become standard practice.

\paragraph{Temporal context and modality choice.}
Both temporal context and modality choice improve detection, but their relative magnitudes differ by scale: at 7--8B, the modality advantage exceeds temporal improvement (\cref{sec:temporal}); at 32B, relative magnitudes are model-dependent. Temporal windowing ($W{=}3$ to $15$) provides consistent improvement regardless of modality, while modality choice requires principled layer selection.

\paragraph{Encoder-conditional advantage.}
Part of the observed advantage may reflect text encoder quality rather than hidden-state informational superiority. At matched 384d dimensionality via PCA (79--92\% variance retained), the precision advantage persists on all four models (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all four model-level precision tests pass Holm-Bonferroni), eliminating capacity gap as the sole explanation. A stronger 1024d encoder closes most of the significance gap (1/4 HB-significant), suggesting a continuum rather than a binary modality distinction. The HS advantage has two separable components: a robust modality effect that survives dimensionality matching, and an encoder-dependent magnitude component that narrows with stronger baselines.

\paragraph{Cross-model divergence and design implications.}
R1-32B and QwQ-32B share architecture but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}), with training method (distillation vs.\ native reasoning) as the likely differentiator; per-model validation is essential. If future work achieves acceptable false positive rates, our results suggest temporal windowing ($W{=}3$--$15$) with principled layer selection as a starting configuration. At 7--8B, HS probes Pareto-dominate text probes, robust across 5 seeds (precision std ${<}8$pp); at 32B ($n{=}19$--$20$), higher variance (R1-32B std $= 12.8$pp) warrants sample-limited interpretation (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). Safe-trace FPR of 39--52\% and the 85.5\% oracle gap (\cref{sec:oracle}) confirm that current probes characterize the commitment phenomenon rather than provide operational detection. PCA capacity control, control task selectivity, and position residualization (HS retains +3.7 to +6.9pp more non-positional signal than text; \cref{app:position_residual}) provide converging evidence that the HS advantage reflects genuine safety representations; direct erasure methods (LEACE; \citealp{belrose2023leace}) remain untested.

